module.exports = [{"title":"Pacific Coastlines Change","description":"Pacific Coastlines beta version product includes coastline change detection since the year 2000 for Pacific Island Country and Territories (PICTs). This product will provide ongoing monitoring of coastline change detection. This provides insights into processes including erosion (where landmass area decreases) and accretion or deposition (where landmass area increases).","methodology":"This draft product is based on a workflow used to map annual coastline locations in Australia and Africa as part of the Digital Earth Australia and Digital Earth Africa projects. For each year all available Landsat satellite data was collected for each location, then filtered to remove clouds and other noise. Annual mosaics were created by calculating the median image values among times of near-average (0-m) sea level using a tidal model. Finally, a water discrimination technique was applied which allowed delineation of exact coastlines at the subpixel level. Results will be further refined with additional noise removal techniques and validated against survey data.","uses":"The coastlines product can be useful for monitoring patterns of movement in annual shoreline locations and determining the velocity and direction of any changes. The product does not attempt to determine causal factors for any observed changes. It may be used in combination with local observations and knowledge, or other external information to better understand possible causes and effects. This product can be applied to analysis around coastal development planning, exposure of economic assets and risk of hazards. The product also sheds light on the correlation between past physical developments, natural disasters and coastal responses to these events.","citation":"Anderson, J. Singh. S. (2023). Coastline change detection - Digital Earth Pacific (DEP). Secretariat of the Pacific Community (SPC).","thumbnailUrl":"./images/apps/coastlines.png","links":[{"title":"Explore Coastlines Change","url":"https://maps.digitalearthpacific.org/#share=s-gdqSovh6xdqQB9gUeSe8"}]},{"title":"Water Observations from Space (WOfS)","description":"Water Observations from Space (WOfS) beta version product for Water Observations from Space (WOfS) is an annual summary of the temporal and spatial extent of surface water over landscapes. In essence, this highlights where water is usually or where it is rarely. The results are visualised to compare points in time spanning over a year, a season or multiple years. The dataset extends back historically to 2013.","methodology":"For each year, all available Landsat data was collected, then filtered to remove clouds. A complex decision tree algorithm was applied, producing a water mask for each date. Annual mosaics were created by calculating the percentage of dates within each year when a particular location contained water.","uses":"The product may be used for a range of applications including monitoring surface water access, detecting intra-annual and interannual changes in water bodies over decades including droughts, flooding, water harvesting and storage. This can be applied to measure reservoir levels in dams, ecological connectivity, surface-groundwater relationships.","citation":"Anderson, J. (2023). Water Observations from Space (WOfS) - Digital Earth Pacific (DEP). Secretariat of the Pacific Community (SPC).","thumbnailUrl":"./images/apps/wofs.png","links":[{"title":"Explore WOfS","url":"https://maps.digitalearthpacific.org/#share=s-a4wwNul0S1MQckdKzLcR"}]},{"title":"Pacific Mangroves Change Detection","description":"Pacific Mangroves beta version product is an extension of the Global Mangrove Watch (GMV v3, 2020). which shows the extent of mangrove ecosystems across Pacific Island Countries and Territories (PICTs). The changes in mangroves extent was further classified into three categories of closed (high-density), open (lower density) and non-mangrove. This was used as the baseline training layer where mangrove categories between 2016 and 2022 were analysed.","methodology":"Mangrove extents and classifications were based on annually derived values of the normalized difference vegetation index (NDVI) applied to a median composite of cloud-masked Sentinel-2 satellite data.","uses":"The DEP Mangroves product supports PICTs to map the extent of mangroves as a requirement to baseline and ongoing Measurement, Reporting and Verification (MRV) for carbon removal projects, REDD+ reporting, national GHG inventory reporting requirements and National Forest Inventories. The applications also extend beyond blue carbon ecosystems to biodiversity and natural shoreline protection mapping.","citation":"Singh. S., Anderson, J. (2023). Mangrove extent - Digital Earth Pacific (DEP). Secretariat of the Pacific Community (SPC).","thumbnailUrl":"./images/apps/mangroves.png","links":[{"title":"Explore Mangroves Change","url":"https://maps.digitalearthpacific.org/#share=s-4VWryod6feOVt2w93RHj"}]},{"title":"Geometric Median and Median Absolute Deviations (GeoMAD) (alpha)","description":"The GeoMAD Sentinel-2 product is a cloud-free composite of satellite data that consolidates multi-temporal imagery into an annual, representative dataset. By integrating geomedian statistics with higher-order measures of variation (MADs), the product enables reliable, consistent monitoring of land and marine environments across the Pacific region, even in regions frequently obscured by clouds. GeoMAD also enables “on-th-fly” visualisation of critical indices around vegetation, water and moisture monitoring, which enables decision making around agriculture, vegetation and climate domain areas.","methodology":"For this product satellite input data is filtered using the Scene Classification Layer (SCL) metadata which removes invalid pixels to improve data quality, specifically excluding pixels identified as saturated or defective (SCL=1), cloud shadows (SCL=3), medium probability clouds (SCL=8), high probability clouds (SCL=9), and thin cirrus clouds (SCL=10). A multi-spectral geomedian is then calculated for each pixel, representing the central value of valid observations collected over the course of a year. In addition, Median Absolute Deviations (MADs) are computed to quantify variation in the data relative to the geomedian, allowing for higher-order statistical analysis. The cleaned and processed imagery is then aggregated using the GeoMAD algorithm, producing an annual cloud-free surface-reflection composite dataset.","uses":"This product can be used for a wide range of practical applications. In disaster response and recovery, it provides a reliable cloud-free baseline for assessing damage and guiding recovery after events such as cyclones and floods. For land cover and vegetation monitoring, the product supports tracking changes in land use, forest cover, and agricultural patterns. In the context of climate resilience, GeoMAD helps monitor long-term environmental shifts, offering critical insights for climate adaptation and mitigation strategies.","citation":"Leith, A. (2024). Geometric Median and Median Absolute Deviations (GeoMAD) - Digital Earth Pacific (DEP). Secretariat of the Pacific Community (SPC).","thumbnailUrl":"./images/apps/geomad.png","links":[{"title":"Explore GeoMAD","url":"https://maps.digitalearthpacific.org/#share=s-7aI1nH2b0d2QnIO3s7om"}]},{"title":"Satellite Derived Bathymetry (SDB) (alpha)","description":"The optical Satellite Derived Bathymetry (SDB) product provides critical depth data for shallow marine environments, reaching depths of up to 30 meters. This dataset supports a range of ocean and coastal management applications by delivering high-resolution bathymetric information in regions that are often difficult or expensive to survey using traditional methods.","methodology":"The SDB product utilises multispectral imagery from the Sentinel-2 satellite, and masks both land and deep water. A machine learning model is then trained on decades of legacy multibeam data collected by SPC, and used to predict depth. Predictions are done on scene-based observations, and the mean of all predictions over a year is used as a final result. Depth estimations are validated using in situ measurements, where available, to ensure accuracy and reliability.","uses":"This product can be used for navigation, marine spatial planning, oceanographic studies and environmental monitoring. Specifically, for mapping fishing grounds, monitoring coastal erosion, modelling coastal inundation and sea level rise, mapping and monitoring benthic habitats to identify and protect sensitive marine habitats and studying ocean currents, tsunamis, and other marine processes.","citation":"Singh. S., Leith, A. (2025). Satellite Derived Bathymetry (SDB) - Digital Earth Pacific (DEP). Secretariat of the Pacific Community (SPC).","thumbnailUrl":"./images/apps/sdb.png","links":[{"title":"Explore SDB","url":"https://maps.digitalearthpacific.org/#share=s-qbLla3VYWC9LfnMEXuMd"}]},{"title":"Pacific Intertidal Model (alpha)","description":"The Intertidal Product maps the dynamic elevation, exposure, and tidal characteristics of the intertidal zone; the transitional area between the highest high tide and the lowest low tide. This zone encompasses sandy beaches, tidal flats, rocky shores, and reef systems. The product provides a consistent, regional scale understanding of how these environments are exposed and submerged over time, offering crucial insights into coastal morphology and hydrodynamic processes.","methodology":"Combined satellite imagery from Sentinel-2 and Landsat 8 for the year 2024 is analysed to identify the exposed intertidal zones, which is the area where coastal water is present or absent across tidal cycles. Using statistical and remote sensing analysis, pixels are classified based on frequency of exposure, which is then correlated with tidal models. The result is a high-resolution (30 m/px) representation of the intertidal topography and exposure.","uses":"This product can support a range of scientific, environmental, and planning efforts, including but not limited to, coastal erosion monitoring, land reclamation assessments, sea-level rise impact studies, biodiversity conservation and benthic habitat mapping.","citation":"Singh. S. (2025). Pacific Intertidal Model - Digital Earth Pacific (DEP). Secretariat of the Pacific Community (SPC).","thumbnailUrl":"./images/apps/intertidal.png","links":[{"title":"Explore Pacific Intertidal Model","url":"https://maps.digitalearthpacific.org/#share=s-1V4PrQjTLk5z0EgaK1ai"}]}]